  "facts": [
    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Horseshoe Lake got its name from the oxbow lake at the town’s eastern tip, where the water body curves like a horseshoe.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horseshoe_Lake%2C_Arkansas",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The lake averages 10 feet deep, tops out at 30 feet, but summer and fall evaporation can lower levels by up to three feet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/horseshoe_lake_arkansas",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 2007 the Horseshoe Lake Drainage and Irrigation Improvement District installed ten pumps and widened the historic Beck‑Snowden canal to keep water levels steady.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/horseshoe_lake_arkansas",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "After the Louisiana Purchase, veterans received land grants, and during the Civil War Isaac Burgett’s home served as a Union hospital in the area.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/horseshoe_lake_arkansas",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The town hosts the Plantation Gallery, showcasing Delta artists, and the historic Snowden House, a Greek Revival estate that appeared in the 1994 film The Client.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/horseshoe_lake_arkansas",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The New Madrid earthquakes of 1811‑1812 split the Mississippi’s tight bend, creating the oxbow lake that surrounds Horseshoe Lake’s fertile bottomlands.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
